---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import {manifest, roots, titles, box, href, compact, exact, basisLabels} from '../lib/data';
import {readFileSync} from 'node:fs';
import {join} from 'node:path';
const gaps=JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(process.cwd(),'public/data/gaps.json'),'utf8')) as Record<string,Array<{id:string;amount_cents:number;basis?:string;why?:string;path?:string[]}>>;
const percent=(n:number)=>`${(n*100).toFixed(1)}%`;
const groups=[...roots,'city-twice'];
---
<Base title="What we could not find"><section class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">Honest limits</p><h1>What we could not find</h1><p class="lede">Some boxes stop while they are still large. That does not mean all the money was paid to one person or company. It means we cannot show a reliable smaller split from the evidence we have.</p></section><section><h2>How much remains in large boxes?</h2><p>These percentages use <strong>absolute dollars in final boxes</strong>. The strict rule measures the size of the box you actually land on. The build rule also treats a group of budgeted positions as small if its per-position rate is under $1 million. That does not open the group into individual people. “Counted twice” is separate from the City's net budget.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Final-box sizes, from the current data export</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Budget and rule</th><th scope="col">Under $1 million</th><th scope="col">$1 million to $10 million</th><th scope="col">$10 million or more</th></tr></thead><tbody>{groups.flatMap(id=>['strict','build'].map(rule=><tr><th scope="row">{titles[id]}: {rule==='strict'?'By the box you reach':'Counting each budgeted position'}</th>{['lt1m','1m_10m','ge10m'].map(bucket=><td>{manifest.coverage?.[id]?.[rule]?.[bucket]!=null?percent(manifest.coverage[id][rule][bucket]):'Not available'}</td>)}</tr>))}</tbody></table></div><h2>Why do boxes stop?</h2><ul><li>A public budget may show one total without listing projects, contracts or people inside it.</li><li>A reserve may not yet have a decided use. We cannot turn a possible project into an exact budget split.</li><li>A payment dataset covers a period, not necessarily the whole year, and a contract is not the same thing as a payment.</li><li>Some totals are truly one item, such as a single vendor or an amount not yet spent. Making smaller boxes would be misleading.</li></ul><p>Research in <cite>tree_gap_audit_3.md</cite> identifies possible next steps: ask budget offices for more detailed records, check later public reports, or attach project lists as context when they cannot be tied exactly to budget lines. Those research counts describe an earlier snapshot. The table and boxes below come from the current export.</p></section>{groups.map(id=><section aria-labelledby={`gaps-${id}`}><h2 id={`gaps-${id}`}>{titles[id]}: large final boxes</h2><p>{(gaps[id]??[]).length.toLocaleString('en-US')} boxes in this export's gaps list. {id==='city-twice'?'This memo branch is not part of the City net headline.':''}</p><ol>{(gaps[id]??[]).slice(0,30).map(g=>{const n=box(g.id);return <li><a href={n?href(n):`/${id==='city-twice'?'city':id}`}>{g.path?.at(-1)??n?.name??g.id}</a> <strong>{compact(g.amount_cents)}</strong> <span class="muted">({exact(g.amount_cents)}, {basisLabels[g.basis??'']??g.basis??'basis not available'})</span>{g.why&&<p>{g.why}</p>}</li>})}</ol><p>Open a box to see its own evidence and explanation.</p></section>)}</Base>
